.TH OCR 1 "1970-1-1" "v1.0" "Custom Scripts Manual"
.SH NAME
OCR \- Performs optical character recognition on a screen region and outputs the text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ocr
[\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-d\fR [\fIdirectory\fR]] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-o\fR] [\fB\-s\fR] [\fB\-h\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ocr
is a wrapper script that captures a screen region using
.MR screenshot 1
and performs OCR using
.MR tesseract 1 .
.PP
The script requires at least one output option (\fB-c\fR, \fB-o\fR, \fB-d\fR, or \fB-n\fR) to be specified.
By default, status notifications are shown for operations unless suppressed with
.BR \-s .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Print the help message and exit.
.TP
.B \-c
Write the recognized text to the system clipboard using
.MR wl-copy 1 .
.TP
.B \-d [\fIdirectory\fR]
Save the recognized text to a file. If a
.I directory
is provided, the file is saved there. Otherwise, it defaults to
.IR ~/OCR .
Files are named using the timestamp format:
.IR ocr_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.txt .
.TP
.B \-n
Show the recognized text in a desktop notification using
.MR notify-send 1 .
Text longer than 100 characters will be truncated.
.TP
.B \-o
Write the recognized text to stdout.
.TP
.B \-s
Suppress status notifications (success/failure messages). Note that this does not affect the
.B \-n
option, which shows the actual OCR output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure due to missing output arguments, missing dependencies, or OCR errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ocr -c -o
Select a screen region, copy text to clipboard, and output to stdout.
.TP
.B ocr -d ~/Documents/OCR
Select a screen region and save the text to the specified directory.
.TP
.B ocr -n -s
Select a screen region and show the text in a notification without status messages.
.TP
.B ocr -c -d
Select a screen region, copy text to clipboard, and save to default OCR directory.
.TP
.B ocr -o | wc -w
Select a screen region, output text to stdout, and count the words.
.SH SEE ALSO
.MR screenshot 1 ,
.MR tesseract 1 ,
.MR wl-copy 1 ,
.MR notify-send 1
.SH NOTES
The script depends on the
.B screenshot
utility for screen capture, which itself uses
.MR grim 1
and
.MR slurp 1
on Wayland compositors.
